WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:05.170
In the world of Hollywood animation, Disney's
pantheon of princess movies are the gold standard

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:06.860
of lighthearted family fun.

00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:11.419
With singing animals, big-eyed heroines, guaranteed
happily-ever-afters, and a nice, comfortable

00:00:11.419 --> 00:00:15.449
G-rating across the board, there's nothing
even remotely dark or disturbing about these

00:00:15.449 --> 00:00:19.480
whimsical films … until you take a peek
at the horrifying tales they're based on.

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:23.740
Here's a look at the creepy stories behind
the Disney princess movies.

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:25.050
Frozen

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:29.380
Frozen broke major ground for Disney as a
feel-good, feminist-friendly entry into its

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:31.070
canon of classic princess fare.

00:00:31.070 --> 00:00:36.140
And to accomplish that, all they had to change
about the source material was...well...everything.

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:40.460
Frozen is based on Hans Christian Andersen's
The Snow Queen, a lengthy meditation on the

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:41.620
nature of evil.

00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:45.640
In the original story, the Snow Queen isn't
a misunderstood teenager struggling to bear

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:50.250
the weight of her epic ice-making powers and
the legacy of some seriously lousy parenting.

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:55.360
Instead, she's a cold, creepy, inhuman villainess
who kidnaps a little boy named Kai, smooches

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:59.920
him to give him amnesia, and then forces him
to do math puzzles all day and night in her

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:00.920
ice palace.

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:01.920
Talk about sadistic!

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:05.930
By the time Kai's sister finds him several
years later, he's a frostbite ridden shadow

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:07.050
of his former self.

00:01:07.050 --> 00:01:10.720
Good luck turning that into a cuddly action
figure.

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:11.720
Cinderella

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:15.570
Like most of Disney's classic animated films,
this dreamy old-school princess story was

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:18.240
a complete nightmare in its original incarnation.

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:21.980
For one thing, Cinderella's father is alive
and well in the fairytale by the Brothers

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:22.980
Grimm.

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:26.550
He's not just there to see how badly she's
being treated, he's complicit in it.

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:31.370
For another, Cinderella in her original incarnation
isn't so much a hapless servant girl as a

00:01:31.370 --> 00:01:36.170
powerful sorceress who inexplicably tolerates
being treated like slime by her family even

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:37.950
though she can conjure magic with her tears.

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:46.940
"A dream, is a wish your heart makes."

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:48.040
And Cinderella's stepsisters?

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:51.280
They don't just embarrass themselves when
they try to pretend they're the owners of

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:55.860
Cinderella's missing slipper — they literally
dismember their feet to make the shoe fit.

00:01:55.860 --> 00:02:00.229
One of the sisters chops off her big toe,
and the other removes a piece of her heel.

00:02:00.229 --> 00:02:03.670
In both cases, the prince only notices that
something is wrong when a bunch of talking

00:02:03.670 --> 00:02:07.229
birds point out to him that his bride's shoe
is filling up with blood.

00:02:07.229 --> 00:02:08.590
And that's not all!

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:12.250
At the very end of the story, the sisters
show up at Cinderella's wedding and walk her

00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:16.389
down the aisle in a shameless last-minute
attempt to curry her favor — at which point

00:02:16.389 --> 00:02:20.280
the talking birds, who have had just about
enough of this social-climbing nonsense, fly

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:22.499
down and peck their eyes out.

00:02:22.499 --> 00:02:24.499
The Little Mermaid

00:02:24.499 --> 00:02:28.650
We all know the story of sweet Ariel, who
fell in love with a human man and got a well-deserved

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:30.730
happy ending with a princely husband.

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:35.189
But while Disney's Little Mermaid lived happily
ever after as a part of your world, the Hans

00:02:35.189 --> 00:02:39.629
Christian Andersen story that the movie was
based on is a much, much darker affair — full

00:02:39.629 --> 00:02:45.349
of dismemberment, murder, existential crises,
and exactly zero reggae jam musical numbers

00:02:45.349 --> 00:02:47.629
composed by a crotchety talking crab.

00:02:47.629 --> 00:02:51.809
"While we devotin', full time to floating
under the sea."

00:02:55.739 --> 00:02:59.959
In the original Little Mermaid, she has to
sacrifice her ability to speak the old-fashioned

00:02:59.959 --> 00:03:02.739
way — by letting the sea witch cut out her
tongue.

00:03:02.739 --> 00:03:07.319
And once on land, despite living in the prince's
palace where she silently, perpetually pines

00:03:07.319 --> 00:03:10.620
for him, the heroine never even comes close
to getting her man.

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:14.299
Instead, he friend-zones her and marries somebody
else.

00:03:14.299 --> 00:03:17.450
That leaves her with two terrible choices:
she can either stab the prince to death on

00:03:17.450 --> 00:03:19.680
his wedding night — or she can kill herself.

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:23.549
Needless to say, she takes the noble option
and throws herself into the sea … at which

00:03:23.549 --> 00:03:27.230
point it turns out that her act of sacrifice
has earned her a place in heaven.

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:32.659
But only after she's forced to spend 300 years
in purgatory as an invisible wind spirit?

00:03:32.659 --> 00:03:35.140
Even Ursula thinks that's going a bit too
far.

00:03:35.140 --> 00:03:36.489
Pocahontas

00:03:36.489 --> 00:03:41.250
Much as we love the Disney take on this old-school
American legend, the true story of Pocahontas

00:03:41.250 --> 00:03:42.980
is mostly just kind of sad.

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:47.739
For one thing, she was just 11 years old when
she saved Captain John Smith's life by convincing

00:03:47.739 --> 00:03:49.459
her father not to execute him.

00:03:49.459 --> 00:03:52.459
The two of them never had a romance.

00:03:52.459 --> 00:03:58.339
Instead the teenage Pocahontas married a fellow
Powhatan, but was taken captive in 1613 by

00:03:58.339 --> 00:03:59.339
the English.

00:03:59.339 --> 00:04:03.079
Although her father tried to negotiate and
bring her back, Pocahontas quickly converted

00:04:03.079 --> 00:04:08.349
to Christianity, changed her name to Rebecca,
and married a tobacco farmer named John Rolfe.

00:04:08.349 --> 00:04:12.849
She ultimately followed Rolfe to England and
died there the following year, at age 20,

00:04:12.849 --> 00:04:16.169
never having seen her father or family again.

00:04:16.169 --> 00:04:17.169
Beauty and the Beast

00:04:17.169 --> 00:04:21.639
Lovely, bookish Belle is one of the most memorable
heroines in Disney history.

00:04:21.639 --> 00:04:25.490
But in Jeanne-Marie Leprince de Beaumont's
Beauty and the Beast, the original story on

00:04:25.490 --> 00:04:29.680
which the movie was based, the circumstances
that lead to her coming to stay with the transfigured

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:31.419
prince are a lot more questionable.

00:04:31.419 --> 00:04:35.460
In the original Beauty and the Beast, Beauty's
father gets on the Beast's bad side, and the

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:40.060
Beast decides that either dear ol' Dad, or
one of his daughters must die as a punishment.

00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:43.539
The father offers himself, but Belle volunteers
instead.

00:04:43.539 --> 00:04:47.330
But Beast can't bring himself to kill her
on account of the butterflies in his stomach.

00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:51.120
He proposes marriage to her every day for
three months before letting her go back to

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:52.710
her dad's house for a visit.

00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:56.610
Beauty returns to the castle to find the Beast
near death — not at the hands of Gaston,

00:04:56.610 --> 00:05:00.370
who doesn't even exist in this version, but
because the Beast has been intentionally starving

00:05:00.370 --> 00:05:02.900
himself as a twisted form of emotional manipulation.

00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:06.960
It works, and Beauty agrees to marry him to
stop him from killing himself.

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:09.440
Now if that isn't true love, I don't know
what is.

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:10.440
Thanks for watching!

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:13.370
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:13.370 --> 00:05:15.790
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

